{"version":1,"ops":[{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1491519406,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDEwMzMxNTQwMzM="},"added":["regression"],"removed":[]},{"type":5,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1491519406,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOkxhYmVsZWRFdmVudDEwMzMxNTQwMzQ="},"added":["annex"],"removed":[]},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1491524459,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI5MjM3MzM5OQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1456#issuecomment-292373399"},"message":"I'm not clear how faecd73f327bee5067998dd6d398033fb09a26a6 broke\ndatalad. Is datalad setting GIT_SSH/GIT_SSH_COMMAND when it runs\ngit-annex?\n\n-- \nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1491530526,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI5MjM5OTAzMw==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1456#issuecomment-292399033"},"message":"yeap.  We set 'GIT_SSH_COMMAND' to `datalad sshrun` (if not already in the environment)","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1491577764,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI5MjU2MzIwMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1456#issuecomment-292563201"},"message":"Yaroslav Halchenko wrote:\n\u003e yeap. We set 'GIT_SSH_COMMAND' to datalad sshrun (if not already in the\n\u003e environment)\n\nAnd your argument parser for datalad sshrun does fall over when passed\nan extra -n as the first parameter?\n\nIf so, I could implement the ANNEX_USE_GIT_SSH=1 thing. At least until\nI can find a way to not need to pass -n when running ssh.\n\n-- \nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"625d4f81fd78f1510c65762d99779f95c491087f"},"timestamp":1491578255,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI5MjU2NTUyMQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1456#issuecomment-292565521"},"message":"Verified in the source to SSHRun.\n\nOf course, you could make that code support the extra leading -n\nparmater and pass it through to ssh. This would fix the breakage too.\n\nSeems to me that datalad might want to make its sshrun be used when\ngit-annex runs ssh. I see some rather ugly looking code in datalad that\nuses annex-ssh-options to make it use the shared connections now, which\nyou would be able to get rid of if you used git-annex's GIT_SSH_COMMAND\nsupport.\n\n-- \nsee shy jo","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"bfb0c87a087f143d5d4fb3c3b303347e581f98d0"},"timestamp":1491579517,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI5MjU3MTI2Ng==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1456#issuecomment-292571266"},"message":"@joeyh yeah -- we didn't support `-n`, added it but it didn't fix the issue(s)... looking into it -- there might be more to the 'story' on our end (#1458) but still -- somehow it worked before and no longer does :-/\n\nyeap -- we were passing via annex-ssh-options since that was the only way AFAIK to reuse the same connection (managed by us) by both annex and datalad... also we did use GIT_SSH_COMMAND to point to our ssh runner already for pure git interactions.  Or what do you mean by \" if you used git-annex's GIT_SSH_COMMAND support.\"?  that if with new functionality in annex we just remove passing control paths via annex-ssh-options?","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1491827945,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI5MjkzNzE2NQ==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1457#issuecomment-292937165"},"message":"I was treating this as Debian's flavor of rfc822, but it didn't actually need much. My example is here: http://docs.datalad.org/en/latest/metadata.html#rfc822-compliant-meta-data The whole thing is inspired by this list http://docs.datalad.org/en/latest/metadata.html#rfc822-compliant-meta-data and amended by a selection of fields from a standard debian/control file. The semantic definitions are mostly provided by https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/DOAP (https://github.com/ewilderj/doap).\n\nMulti-value fields are separated by comma (as in email headers, or debian/control). I see no reason to depart from that (and having to write a parser as a consequence).","files":null},{"type":3,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1491838908,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDEyOklzc3VlQ29tbWVudDI5Mjk4OTEyMg==","github-url":"https://github.com/datalad/datalad/issues/1457#issuecomment-292989122"},"message":"Thought about the general approach a bit more. I think it is very much related to another issue I face in day to day work, and could be solved together. See #1462","files":null},{"type":4,"author":{"id":"cf7a0bdf96b5195bf3052274368f463c7410f7a8"},"timestamp":1518269944,"metadata":{"github-id":"MDExOkNsb3NlZEV2ZW50MTQ2Nzk5OTgwMA=="},"status":2}]}